  "derivation_sha256": "c0c515a76279e7fe765feb567e00d0a5a50c0f24bbc775c0e4d3aa75fe14fe36",
  "theorem_contract": {
    "quadratic_gauss_spectral_flatness": "For cubic phases P(x) = a x^3 + b x^2 + c x, the shift difference is an exact quadratic chirp. By quadratic Gauss sums, |F_hat(k)|^2 == 1/p identically for all k in Z_p. Standard Quantum Fourier Sampling yields I(s : K) == 0.0 bits identically.",
    "dirac_needle_alignment_landscape": "The candidate de-chirping alignment function S(alpha) is 1.0 at alpha = 3 a s mod p and 1/p everywhere else. By BBBV, finding the correct chirp rate requires Omega(sqrt{p}) = 2^{Omega(n/2)} quantum queries and Omega(p) = 2^{Omega(n)} classical evaluations.",
    "bombieri_weil_anti_concentration": "For polynomial phases of degree d >= 4, the shift difference has degree >= 3. By the Bombieri-Weil bound, P(k) <= (d-2)^2 / p for all frequencies k. Total variation distance vanishes as O(d / sqrt{p}) -> 0, and single-copy mutual information vanishes as O(d^4 / p) -> 0 bits.",
    "multi_copy_sieve_cross_coordinate_decoherence": "Non-linear polynomial phases generate cross-coordinate phases Phi(x_1, x_2; s) = (x_1 - x_2) R(x_1, x_2; s). Tracing out coordinate sums destroys off-diagonal coherence as Tr_v(omega^{3 a s u v}) = p delta_{u, 0}, ruling out Kuperberg/Regev quantum sieve speedups.",
    "algebraic_phase_trilemma": "Every algebraic phase family falls into trivial linear (Sector I), classically dequantized quadratic (Sector II), or exponentially flat / needle-in-a-haystack non-linear (Sector III)."
  },
  "headline_metrics": {
